Subject: disable utils.update_submodules()

src/experiments/setup.py:23 calls utils.update_submodules() unconditionally,
which runs `git submodule update --init --recursive` across the whole tree.

Observed on pep-star02, 2026-11-05: a plain

    src/experiments/setup.py --schemes "taova indigo pcc vivace"

went straight into cloning https://github.com/google/proto-quic.git -- a
Chromium fork of several GB that docs/decisions/002 deliberately does not
fetch, and which nothing in this project uses. Worse, the same command checks
out over third_party/{genericCC,pcc,vivace,indigo}, which would overwrite the
sources patched by 0001/0002 and the binaries compiled from them, reverting
both patches with no sign that anything happened.

Only setup.py calls it; src/experiments/test.py never does. So it becomes a
loud no-op rather than being removed, and setup.py keeps working for
everything else.

Applied by docker/apply-patches.py :: no_submodule_update().

--- a/src/helpers/utils.py
+++ b/src/helpers/utils.py
@@
 def update_submodules():
-    cmd = 'git submodule update --init --recursive'
-    check_call(cmd, shell=True)
+    # ccbench-remote: disabled on purpose. The submodules this image
+    # needs are already checked out, patched and compiled; an
+    # `update --init --recursive` here would fetch gigabytes we do
+    # not use (proto-quic) and check out over the patched sources.
+    sys.stderr.write('ccbench-remote: update_submodules() disabled; '
+                     'the tree in this image is already prepared\n')
+    return
